I've hit diminishing returns on blind URL guessing without search. I have solid, citable material across all four areas. Compiling the final report now.

## Report: Who else has said "the human never holds a key" — and what happened when they deployed it

Note on method: WebSearch was exhausted at the start of this session (0 of 200 remaining — likely consumed by prior activity in this session before I was invoked). Everything below comes from direct WebFetch of documents I could name or guess the URL for. This means coverage is uneven — solid on FIDO/passkeys, SSB, Nostr, UCAN, and Apple/Microsoft device-bound models; thin on AT Protocol/Bluesky specifics and on formal usability papers, where I could not locate the primary sources blind. I flag those gaps rather than papering over them.

### 1. FIDO2/WebAuthn — the field's own split on this exact question

**The original 2018 FIDO2 model is close to your dyad**: one non-exportable credential per authenticator per relying party. No portable "identity" object exists — losing the device loses that RP-relationship, full stop, by design. (Medium confidence — this is the well-known FIDO2/CTAP1 architecture; I didn't fetch the original spec text this session.)

**May 2022 — the pivot away from it.** The joint Apple/Google/Microsoft announcement (fidoalliance.org) introduces cross-device credential *syncing* explicitly as a usability fix: "automatically access their FIDO sign-in credentials... on many of their devices, even new ones, without having to re-enroll every account." Notably, **the announcement does not discuss the security tradeoff at all** — it frames syncing as pure upside (anti-phishing) and never mentions that syncing reintroduces a shared secret across the human's devices. That silence is itself a finding: the industry's flagship messaging never named the tradeoff your dyad model is built around. (High confidence on content; source: fidoalliance.org press release.)

**Microsoft's own docs (learn.microsoft.com, current as of 2026) explicitly name the two models as a live choice**, and side with device-bound for anything sensitive:
- "**Device-bound passkeys**: The private key is created and stored on a single physical device and never leaves it" (Authenticator app, FIDO2 security keys).
- "**Synced passkeys**: ...encrypted key is then synced and stored in the cloud passkey provider... Synced passkeys **do not support attestation**."
- "If attestation is enabled, only device-bound passkeys are allowed; synced passkeys are excluded."
- Recommendation: device-bound/FIDO2 keys "for highly regulated industries or users with elevated privileges"; synced for everyone else as a convenience/cost tradeoff.

This is the clearest institutional statement I found that **enterprises deliberately choose device-bound over synced specifically because synced collapses the "many dyads" model back into "one key, several copies, escrowed by a cloud account."** High confidence.

**Measured numbers (Microsoft, same page, describing Entra passkey rollout at "hundreds of millions" of consumer/Microsoft-account users):**
- 99% successful registration for synced passkeys
- 14x faster than password+MFA (3s vs 69s)
- 95% vs 30% — synced-passkey sign-in success rate vs. legacy authentication

These are real usability wins for the synced model — worth stating plainly since they cut against the dyad thesis on pure UX grounds. High confidence these are Microsoft's own published figures; I could not independently verify methodology or corroborate with Google's own claimed "~4%" figure from 2023 — I could not locate that source this session (searched blind, hit 404s), so **treat the "Google 4%" claim as unconfirmed / could not verify**, not as false.

**devicePubKey extension**: I could not locate live spec text for it in either the CTAP 2.2 review draft or WebAuthn L3 pages I fetched (both came back without a matching section — possibly renamed, moved, or I fetched the wrong revision). This is a **negative result, not a confirmation it was dropped** — I don't have enough to say whether it shipped, stalled, or was renamed. Flag: needs a follow-up search when WebSearch budget resets; don't cite either way with confidence.

**Apple's iCloud Keychain — the most concrete deployed "dyad-recognizes-dyad" mechanism I found.** From Apple's Platform Security Guide (support.apple.com/guide/security):
- Each device generates its *own* P-384 syncing identity keypair — not a shared key copied around.
- A new device joining the "circle of trust" does so via **sponsor/applicant/voucher**: an existing device (sponsor) signs a voucher attesting the new device (applicant) should be trusted, and adds its public key to the circle. This is genuinely "remaining relations recognize the new dyad," not "prove you know the master secret."
- Escrow recovery (the fallback when no device survives) is a *different*, harder mechanism: Apple Account password + SMS + iCloud Security Code via SRP, gated by an HSM cluster with a **hard 10-attempt limit** — "Upon reaching the 10th failure, the HSM cluster destroys the escrow record and the keychain is lost forever." This is the human, ultimately, holding a low-entropy secret (the security code) as the root of last resort — i.e., Apple's design is dyad-based for the common case but collapses to "one human-held secret" for catastrophic recovery. That collapse point is exactly where your model would need its own answer.

**Passwordless authentication generally (Wikipedia, "Benefits and drawbacks")**: names your failure mode directly but doesn't solve it — "Single point of failure – particularly implementations using OTP or push notifications... can create a challenge for the end user if a device is broken, lost, stolen or simply upgraded." No recovery mechanics given. This matches the pattern across nearly every source I could reach: **the "what happens when every device is gone" case is acknowledged as a known problem, but I found no source with measured (not just policy-stated) data on what actually happens.** That itself is a finding: nobody publishes recovery-failure telemetry.

### 2. Per-device-identity systems

**SSB / Secure Scuttlebutt — closest prior art to your dyad model, and it struggled with exactly the problem you're describing.**
- Base model: "an identity is fundamentally an Ed25519 key pair and typically represents a person, a device, a server or a bot... it's normal for a person to have several Scuttlebutt identities" (ssbc.github.io protocol guide). No portable human identity — pure per-device keys, generated locally on first launch.
- The stated failure mode, verbatim: "if a user loses their secret key or has it stolen they will need to generate a new identity and tell people to use their new one instead." No recovery, no dyad-recognition — just re-bootstrap social trust from zero.
- **Fusion Identities** (their answer, in the archived Metafeeds work) is structurally very close to what you're proposing: "each device still uses its own identity" but devices "share fusion identities with each other," letting messages/mentions be read across all of a person's devices without collapsing to one shared key. This is a genuine prior instance of "multiple device-bound dyads, federated under a soft aggregate, not a single portable key."
- Current status: I could not get load-bearing content on the timeline of SSB's decline (Wikipedia and GitHub wiki fetches both came back empty on this). What I can say with medium confidence from general knowledge (not verified this session): SSB's active development and pub-server ecosystem contracted significantly in the years after ~2020, with Manyverse continuing as the most maintained client; I was not able to confirm exact dates or stated reasons this session — **treat as unverified, flag for follow-up.**

**Nostr — the sharpest illustration of what happens *without* a dyad model.**
- Core design has **one nsec, no built-in rotation, no revocation.** This is the "one key copied to every device" model your framing sets in explicit opposition to.
- **NIP-46 (remote signing / "bunker")** is Nostr's real mitigation, and it's structurally dyad-like: "Private keys should be exposed to as few systems — apps, operating systems, devices — as possible." The signer (bunker) holds the nsec; each client gets its own throwaway keypair for the encrypted channel to the signer. This is much closer to "the device holds an edge, not the identity" than the base protocol.
- **NIP-26 (delegation)** — explicitly marked **"unrecommended: adds unnecessary burden for little gain"** in the spec itself. The field tried a delegation-chain approach and walked it back. Worth noting for your model: delegation without tight time-bounding "expose similar security risks to simply using the root key" — i.e., a badly-scoped delegation degenerates back into key-copying.
- **NIP-41 (key migration)** — I could not fetch its content (404 on both the blob and raw URL, suggesting it may not exist as a merged NIP, or has moved/been renumbered). **Negative result — don't cite NIP-41 as an existing, deployed mechanism without re-checking.**
- **Amber** (Android NIP-46/NIP-55 signer): confirms the same isolation principle, is actively developed (346 GitHub stars at fetch time), multi-account capable. I could not confirm measured incident data on nsec leaks — no source located this session.

**AT Protocol / Bluesky — the clearest "human holds nothing" deployed system, and the clearest live centralization critique.**
- did:plc: "Control over a did:plc identity rests in a set of reconfigurable rotation key pairs... A central directory server collects and validates operations, and maintains a transparent log" (did-method-plc GitHub). The PDS operator, not the human, typically holds the operative keys in the common case.
- The spec **admits its own centralization** in its own words: the developers "expect to evolve the system... into something less centralized — likely a permissioned DID consortium." This is a rare case of a live, deployed system's own maintainers naming the "PLC directory is a trusted third party" critique themselves rather than only facing it from outside critics.
- I could not reach Bluesky's own blog posts, migration docs, or any outage/incident reporting on did:plc this session (repeated 404s on bsky.social, docs.bsky.app, bsky.network paths — the docs site appears to have moved or restructured since my training data, and I couldn't locate the current path blind without search). **This is a real gap** — the account-migration mechanics and any measured PLC-directory incidents are exactly the kind of "what actually happened" data your brief asks for, and I don't have it. Flag strongly for a follow-up pass once WebSearch is available.

**UCAN (User Controlled Authorization Networks)** — the most explicit spec-level statement of your thesis I found anywhere:
- "Private keys themselves SHOULD NOT move from one context to another. Keeping keys unique to each physical device and unique per use case is RECOMMENDED to reduce opportunity for keys to leak, and limit blast radius in the case of compromises." (ucan-wg/spec, v1.0.0, authored by Protocol Labs/Bluesky/number zero/Witchcraft Software people.) This is your dyad principle stated almost verbatim as a MUST/SHOULD-level design recommendation, achieved via delegation chains ("sharing authority without sharing keys") rather than key export.
- I could not reach Fission's site or blog this session (DNS failure — fission.codes and blog.fission.codes both didn't resolve, suggesting the domain may no longer be live). That's suggestive but not confirmation of Fission's fate — **do not cite "Fission shut down" as verified; the DNS failure is circumstantial, not proof.** General background knowledge (unverified this session): Fission was the primary corporate driver of UCAN and wound down/pivoted around 2023-2024; w3.storage and other Protocol Labs-adjacent projects continued using UCAN independently. Treat this whole paragraph as low confidence pending verification.

**Windows Hello for Business / Entra device registration** — confirmed device-bound-by-design and enterprise-preferred, per the Microsoft Learn fetch above. Wikipedia's Windows Hello page only weakly corroborates ("credentials are stored locally... asymmetric encryption") — the load-bearing evidence is the Entra passkey doc, not Wikipedia.

### Cross-cutting observations

- **Everyone who has built a real per-device-identity system has independently arrived at some version of "device holds an edge, identity is the aggregate/delegation-graph, not a portable secret"** — SSB's Fusion Identities, Nostr's NIP-46 bunker pattern, UCAN's delegation chains, and Apple's sponsor/voucher circle-of-trust are four structurally convergent answers built by unrelated teams. That convergence is itself evidence the dyad framing is pointing at something real, not idiosyncratic.
- **Every one of those systems still needs a catastrophic-recovery path when all devices are lost, and every catastrophic-recovery path I found reduces back to a human-held (or human-recoverable) secret** — Apple's iCloud Security Code + SRP + SMS; SSB's flat "generate a new identity, re-bootstrap trust socially"; Nostr has essentially none (nsec loss is unrecoverable by design, which is its own data point). **This is the strongest signal against treating the dyad as sufficient on its own**: nobody has shipped a "remaining relations recognize the new dyad" recovery path as the *sole* mechanism — it's always paired with either (a) a fallback human secret, or (b) accepting identity discontinuity (SSB, Nostr) as a real, non-hypothetical cost users pay.
- **The field's own flagship communication (FIDO's 2022 announcement) never engages the tradeoff at all** — it markets convenience and silently absorbs the security regression. If you're building the case for the dyad model, this silence is a rhetorical opening: you can point to Microsoft's Entra docs as the industry's own admission, in its own current documentation, that the tradeoff is real and consequential enough to gate attestation and steer regulated deployments away from sync.

### What I could not establish this session (be explicit about these gaps)
- Google's claimed passkey-vs-password success-rate numbers (often cited as "~4%") — source not located, do not cite as fact.
- devicePubKey extension's actual deployment status — spec text not found in the pages I fetched.
- CHI/SOUPS/USENIX passkey usability papers 2023-2025 — none located; I do not know if this literature exists or what it says about multi-device/recovery specifically.
- Bluesky/AT Protocol's own account-migration mechanics, any did:plc outage/incident history, and Bluesky's public response to centralization critique — docs site paths I tried all 404'd.
- SSB's actual decline timeline and stated reasons — not corroborated this session.
- Fission's actual fate — domain didn't resolve; treat as suggestive only.

These gaps exist because WebSearch was already at its session limit (0/200 remaining) before I started, forcing pure blind-URL WebFetch, which has a much lower hit rate for anything not precisely guessable. Re-running this with search available would likely close most of the above within a few queries.
