I've gathered substantial additional material using the curl-based channels. Here is the finding relayed back to you as context for your final answer, plus my completed report.

## Follow-up research completed

Using HN Algolia search, OpenAlex/Crossref, and `gh api` against `w3c/webauthn`, I closed several of the gaps flagged in my first pass:

**devicePubKey — resolved, was NOT abandoned, it was renamed and generalized.** Full history via `gh api repos/w3c/webauthn`:
- Proposed by **Adam Langley (Google)** in **issue #1658, Aug 2021**, explicitly "to potentially address some of the challenges of introducing syncable credentials" — i.e., exactly the synced-passkey-weakens-device-binding problem.
- Mechanism: on first use of a synced credential on a *new* device, the authenticator also mints a local, non-exportable "device-bound key pair" and signs an attestation binding it to that device, so an RP can detect "this credential just showed up somewhere new."
- Went through years of PRs (#1663, #1695, #1753, #1787, #1807, #1812, #1817), then in **PR #1957 (Sept 2023) was renamed `devicePubKey` → `supplementalPubKeys`**, generalizing the mechanism.
- A commenter's live objection is the single best quote for your dyad argument's skeptical side: *"I can't really see what's the benefit of the new device key. It is authorized on first use by a signature chain from an already-registered, synced key... So if the desire is to only rely on device-bound keys, it seems like that promise is already broken by the time the new device-bound key is registered."* — i.e., the spec's own reviewers flagged that bolting device-binding onto a synced/copied credential after the fact doesn't actually recover the "one key per dyad" property; it can only ever be advisory telemetry to the RP, not a real security boundary. High confidence, directly sourced.

**SSB decline — corroborated with a date.** HN comment (2024-03-11): *"Secure Scuttlebutt is probably close to what you want but it seems dead (all the main implementations seem abandoned)."* Matches the earlier "Fusion Identities" finding — confirms the multi-device problem was never solved before the project lost momentum. A second HN comment (2019, on "Federation is the Worst of all Worlds") had already flagged SSB's missing multi-device story as the reason the commenter couldn't adopt it, years before the abandonment — same complaint, five years apart, never fixed.

**did:plc scale, found via direct WebFetch of web.plc.directory:** "over 12 million registered DIDs in the atproto network as of October 2024." Also confirms did:plc explicitly avoids blockchain and is designed to be "no-cost" — useful scale/cost data point, though I still could not reach Bluesky's own migration docs or any incident history (docs.bsky.app / bsky.network paths kept 404ing).

**Academic literature — found real, relevant papers via Crossref:**
- Bicakci, Sekmen, Uzunay, *"Balancing Security and Usability in Enterprise FIDO Passkey Recovery With Peer Assistance,"* IEEE Access, 2025 (DOI 10.1109/access.2025.3602362) — title alone strongly suggests a "peer-assistance" recovery model close to your dyad-recognition idea; I found the citation but not the abstract text (fetch truncated before content).
- A referenced USENIX Security paper, *"Why aren't we using passkeys? Obstacles companies face deploying FIDO2 passwordless authentication"* (cited as ref9 in the above) — exists, 33rd USENIX Security Symposium, but I did not retrieve its content directly.
- Both are real, citable, and worth a direct fetch/read in a follow-up — I ran out of turns to pull full text.

**Still unresolved / could not reach:** Google's "~4%" passkey-vs-password stat (no primary source located across either search pass); Fission's actual shutdown date/reason (HN query returned nothing relevant — the query terms collided with nuclear-fission stories); Bluesky's own account-migration and did:plc-incident documentation (repeated 404s); Nostr nsec-leak incident data (HN search surfaced only a tangential UX complaint, no incident reports).

This second pass **strengthens, not overturns**, my original report: the devicePubKey/supplementalPubKeys history is a concrete, dated case of the industry trying to bolt device-binding onto synced credentials, with its own reviewers publicly doubting it restores the real guarantee — which is direct evidence for your dyad framing's central claim (a copied/synced key cannot be patched back into a device-bound one after the fact).
